# DESCRIPTION

`time` returns the seconds since the Unix epoch by the realtime clock, and stores them in <out> if it is not null.

`clock_gettime` stores the time of <clock> in <ts>.
The clocks, with Linux's numbers, are:

| Clock | Value | Reads |
| `CLOCK_REALTIME` | 0 | UTC, from the clock the process was given |
| `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` | 1 | croi's monotonic clock |
| `CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW` | 4 | the same |
| `CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE` | 6 | the same |
| `CLOCK_BOOTTIME` | 7 | the same |

The realtime clock is set from the machine's real-time clock by the time service.
A process given no such clock reads the monotonic clock as realtime.
Any other <clock>, or a null <ts>, fails with `EINVAL`.

`clock_getres` stores a resolution of 1 nanosecond for each of those clocks in <res>, which may be null.

`nanosleep` sleeps the calling thread for <duration> on the monotonic clock.
A negative number of seconds, or nanoseconds outside 0 to 999999999, fails with `EINVAL`.
Nothing interrupts the sleep, since there are no signals, so <remaining> is never written.
A duration beyond the clock's range sleeps forever.

`clock_nanosleep` sleeps the calling thread for <request> on <clock>, one of those above, or with `TIMER_ABSTIME` in <flags> until <clock> reads <request>; a time already past returns at once.
A realtime deadline becomes a monotonic one when the sleep starts, so a change of the UTC clock during it doesn't move it.
As POSIX has it, it returns the error number rather than setting `errno`: `EINVAL` for another clock, another flag, or a time out of range as for `nanosleep`.
<remaining> is never written.

The others return 0 on success (`time` the time) and -1 with `errno` set on error.

## Formatting a time

`strftime` writes <format> with the fields of <tm> to <s>, at most <max> bytes with the terminating NUL, and returns the bytes written before the NUL, or 0 if they didn't fit (the contents of <s> are then undefined).
Every conversion of C23 is there, in the C locale: names in English, `%c` as `%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Y`, `%x` as `%m/%d/%y`, `%X` as `%H:%M:%S`, `%p` as `AM` or `PM`; `%g`, `%G` and `%V` are ISO 8601's week-based year and week; `%z` is `tm_gmtoff` as `+hhmm` and `%Z` is `tm_zone`, or `UTC` when it is null.
The `E` and `O` modifiers are accepted and change nothing.
A name whose field is out of range prints `?`; an unknown conversion is copied as written.
`strftime_l` takes a locale object (locale(2)) and does the same.

## sys/time.h

`gettimeofday` stores the realtime clock, as `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)` reads it, in <tv>; <zone> is ignored.
`utimes` fails with `ENOSYS`: no file's times can be set.

